          kind: "paragraph",
          body:
            "A model API is a network service like any other — and everything the earlier courses taught about networked services applies without modification: it can be slow, it can fail, it costs money per byte, it can be abused, and it must never be reachable from a place it shouldn't be. What is *new* is that its outputs are probabilistic: the same request can produce different answers, so correctness is a distribution rather than a value, and the engineering must handle \"wrong but fluent\" as a first-class outcome.\n\n**Why the call belongs on your server, always** — the three reasons, each with its incident:\n\n1. **The key never ships.** Anything in client code is public: bundle inspection, source maps, a curious teenager with dev tools. A provider key in a browser is a credit card taped to the window — and providers' abuse detection will burn your account for someone else's usage regardless of whose fault it is. The browser calls *your* API; your API calls the model.\n2. **The call needs your context.** The prompt that matters includes things only the server should know or should assemble: the user's data, their entitlements, the conversation history from *your* database, rate limits keyed to *their* account. Client-side calls bypass all of that.\n3. **Rate limits, caching, logging and shaping are policy, and policy lives server-side.** Per-user quotas, prompt caching, output filtering, audit logs — a client-side architecture has nowhere to put any of it.\n\nThe shape that follows: the client sends *intent* (a question, a draft request), your API validates and authenticates as it would for any endpoint (the validation and auth lessons, unchanged), assembles the prompt with server-side context, calls the provider, **inspects the result**, and returns only what the UI needs. The model call is an implementation detail inside your service boundary — not an architecture.\n\n**The chat API, demystified.** Modern model APIs are a message list in, text (and optionally structured data) out:\n\n```json\nPOST /v1/messages\n{\n  \"model\": \"claude-sonnet-4-5\",\n  \"system\": \"You are the support assistant for … Answer only from the notes below. …\",\n  \"messages\": [\n    { \"role\": \"user\",      \"content\": \"How do I reset my password?\" },\n    { \"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": \"Use the Forgot password link…\" },\n    { \"role\": \"user\",      \"content\": \"And if the email never arrives?\" }\n  ],\n  \"max_tokens\": 600,\n  \"temperature\": 0.2,\n  \"stop_sequences\": [\"END\"]\n}\n→ { \"content\": [{ \"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"…\" }],\n    \"stop_reason\": \"end_turn\",\n    \"usage\": { \"input_tokens\": 812, \"output_tokens\": 143 } }\n```\n\nEvery field is a design decision: **`system`** sets standing behaviour (the *prompts-as-product* lesson's territory — it is your product's constitution); **`messages`** is the conversation, where *your* history is the context you must manage (see below); **`max_tokens`** caps output — always set it, because unbounded generation is unbounded cost; **`temperature`** trades diversity for consistency (0–0.3 for extraction, classification, anything where one right answer exists; higher for brainstorming where variation is the point — it never makes a wrong answer *more right*, only different); **`stop_sequences`** give you hard terminators; and **`usage`** returns the token accounting you will need for every cost decision later.\n\n**History is context engineering, not recording.** The naive client sends the whole transcript every turn — costs grow linearly with conversation length, then hit context limits, then the model's attention diffuses across old irrelevant turns (attention is not free; long contexts *degrade* quality — \"lost in the middle\" is real). The server-side discipline: decide what each turn actually needs — the last *n* messages verbatim, an earlier summary for the rest, retrieved documents for this question — and assemble that. **You are not sending a conversation; you are composing the smallest context that answers the question.** That single reframing is what separates a demo chat from a product: cost, latency and quality all improve when history is curated rather than dumped.\n\n**Streaming** changes the user's experience rather than the work: the response arrives as server-sent events (SSE) chunk by chunk, so text appears as it generates — perceived latency drops from \"time to complete answer\" to \"time to first token\", often from seconds to hundreds of milliseconds *with identical model behaviour*. Implementation reality: the server holds the provider connection open and pipes events to the client (the async lesson's streaming, at app scale); the client appends chunks (render per chunk cheaply — the DOM lesson's rules, at ten-per-second instead of one); and the *failure modes multiply*: connection drops mid-stream (the client must handle partial content honestly — show it as incomplete, offer regenerate), and the server must still enforce timeouts and cancellation (user navigates away → abort the provider call so you stop paying for a response nobody will read — the AbortController discipline again).\n\n**Timeouts, retries and the refusal class.** Provider calls fail in characteristic ways: timeouts (the model is slow under load — your timeout needs headroom *and* a bound; 30–60s for long generations is normal, which is exactly why streaming exists), rate limits (`429` with `Retry-After` — honour it with jittered backoff, the resilience lesson verbatim), overload (`5xx` — retryable with the same discipline), and **content refusals** — the model declining to answer (provider policy) or returning an empty/`stop_reason: refusal` response. Refusals are *not errors to retry*: they are outcomes to *render* — \"I can't help with that\" is a legitimate product state, and retrying it wastes tokens and sometimes escalates into an infinite loop of the same refusal. Map every outcome to a UI state: streaming text, complete, empty, refused, rate-limited, timeout, failed. **Seven states, each designed** — the data-fetching lesson's discipline, now with two new rows.\n\n**Structured outputs and their limits.** When the consumer is *your program* rather than a person, ask for a schema-constrained shape (JSON with a schema, tool-call arguments, or strict mode where the provider guarantees validity): the model's output is then guaranteed parseable, which removes a whole class of glue code. But structure ≠ truth: a perfectly-shaped JSON can contain a hallucinated value. Validation now runs at *two* boundaries — the shape (schema) and the content (your domain rules: does this course id exist? is this price a number you have?) — the normalisation lesson with an AI-shaped input.\n\n**Tokens are the meter.** Everything costs in tokens: input (prompt + context) and output (generation). Pricing is per-million tokens with asymmetry (output typically 3–5× input), so the two levers are obvious: *send less* (curated context, prompt caching for repeated prefixes — providers discount cached reads substantially) and *generate less* (`max_tokens`, tighter instructions — \"answer in three sentences\" is a cost control as much as a style choice). Log `usage` on every call per feature and per user (bounded labels — the observability lesson's cardinality rule); the cost dashboard you build here is what makes the evals-and-cost lesson's budget meaningful rather than theoretical.",
