          heading: "Definition: the server-side AI endpoint, properly built",
          kind: "definition",
          body:
            "One endpoint, every obligation visible — this is the shape every AI feature in this course will reuse:\n\n```ts\n// POST /api/ai/assistant — the whole service boundary, annotated.\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst AskSchema = z.object({\n  question: z.string().trim().min(1).max(2_000),      // 1. size-bounded input\n  conversationId: z.string().uuid().optional(),       // 2. server-held history\n});\n\nexport async function POST(req: Request) {\n  // 3. identity + entitlement BEFORE any model spend\n  const user = await requireSession(req);\n  if (!ownsProgram(user, \"vibe-coding\")) return json(403, { code: \"PROGRAM_REQUIRED\" });\n\n  // 4. validate at the edge (the validation lesson — still applies to prompts)\n  const parsed = AskSchema.safeParse(await req.json());\n  if (!parsed.success) return validationError(parsed.error);\n  const { question } = parsed.data;\n\n  // 5. rate limit per user (tokens AND requests — see below)\n  const budget = await consumeAiBudget(user.id, { requests: 1, estTokens: 700 });\n  if (!budget.ok) return json(429, { code: \"AI_QUOTA\", retryAfter: budget.retryAfter });\n\n  // 6. compose context SERVER-side: history (curated) + retrieved notes\n  const history = await loadRecentTurns(parsed.data.conversationId, user.id, { maxMessages: 12 });\n  const notes = await retrieveNotes(question, user.id, { topK: 5 });   // the RAG lesson\n\n  // 7. call with timeout + bounded output + explicit refusal handling\n  const controller = new AbortController();\n  const timer = setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), 45_000);\n  try {\n    const response = await provider.messages.create({\n      model: process.env.AI_MODEL,\n      system: SYSTEM_PROMPT,                 // the product constitution (lesson 2)\n      messages: [...history, { role: \"user\", content: compose(question, notes) }],\n      max_tokens: 600,                       // cost ceiling per call\n      temperature: 0.2,\n    }, { signal: controller.signal });\n\n    const block = response.content.find((c) => c.type === \"text\");\n    const text = block?.text?.trim() ?? \"\";\n    const usage = response.usage;            // 8. the meter\n    await recordAiUsage({ userId: user.id, feature: \"assistant\",\n      inputTokens: usage.input_tokens, outputTokens: usage.output_tokens });\n\n    // 9. refusal / empty are OUTCOMES, not errors\n    if (response.stop_reason === \"refusal\" || !text) {\n      return json(200, { answer: null, state: \"refused\",\n        message: \"I can't help with that one — try rephrasing, or ask about the course material.\" });\n    }\n    // 10. inspect before return: does it contain disallowed content / leaked\n    //     context? (the safety lesson's output filter — a cheap check here)\n    if (containsBlockedPattern(text)) return json(200, { answer: null, state: \"filtered\" });\n\n    await saveTurn(parsed.data.conversationId, user.id, question, text);  // history you control\n    return json(200, { answer: text, state: \"complete\",\n      usage: { in: usage.input_tokens, out: usage.output_tokens } });\n  } catch (err) {\n    // 11. classify: timeout / rate-limit / provider-down / aborted-by-user\n    if (err.name === \"AbortError\") return json(499, { code: \"CLIENT_CANCELLED\" });\n    if (isRateLimited(err))   return json(429, { code: \"PROVIDER_BUSY\", retryAfter: err.retryAfter });\n    if (isTimeout(err))       return json(504, { code: \"AI_TIMEOUT\" });   // UI: retry offer\n    log.error({ err, feature: \"assistant\" });                              // requestId from middleware\n    return json(500, { code: \"AI_UNAVAILABLE\" });                          // UI: graceful fallback text\n  } finally {\n    clearTimeout(timer);\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**Reading the annotations as a checklist** — each number is a failure that exists without it: (1) an unbounded question is a cost attack *and* a context overflow; (3) calling the model before authorising is burning money for non-owners and is exactly the \"client can't be the gate\" rule at AI prices; (5) per-user budgeting is what makes \"AI feature\" sustainable — requests *and* estimated tokens, because one user's thousand-token questions are not another's twenty-word ones; (6) history and retrieval assembled server-side is the context-engineering principle *and* the privacy boundary (only *their* notes are retrieved); (7–9) timeout, classification and refusal-as-outcome — the seven states; (10) output inspection before the user sees it — the safety lesson's first layer at the cheapest possible point; (11) a failure taxonomy mapped to UI states, so a provider outage degrades *this feature* while the product keeps working (the resilience lesson's graceful degradation — an AI feature that takes your app down with it has failed the architecture review).\n\n**Two numbers to log per call** — feature, user, tokens, latency, outcome (`complete|refused|filtered|timeout|rate_limited|error`). Those six dimensions are the entire dashboard the later lessons need: cost per feature (evals-and-cost), refusal rates (safety), latency percentiles (the same monitoring lesson, now with `feature=\"assistant\"`). Bounded labels, the observable design from earlier — the AI layer is not an exception to the instrumentation rules, it is a new value for the same rules.",
